# 3. Recreated same user
useradd -m -s /bin/bash mohit
# 4. Checked file
ls -l /home/node.sh
# -rwxr-xr-x 1 mohit mohit 69 Nov 11 04:55 node.sh
The file still showed mohit mohit with same permissions. I thought deleting the user would delete permissions. It didn't.

What I Learned
1. Linux Stores UID, Not Name
ls -l shows you a name, but the file system stores a number.

File node.sh does NOT store:

owner = mohit
It stores:

owner UID = 1000
group GID = 1000
permissions = -rwxr-xr-x (755)
/etc/passwd is just a phonebook that translates:

1000 -> mohit
Delete the user = delete the phonebook entry. The file still has 1000 written on it.

2. Proof: The Ghost UID
If you check between delete and recreate:

userdel mohit
ls -l /home/node.sh
# -rwxr-xr-x 1 1000 1000 node.sh
No name, just 1000. Because no user with UID 1000 exists anymore.

Recreate user:

useradd -m -s /bin/bash mohit
# Linux gives first free UID = 1000 again
ls -l /home/node.sh
# -rwxr-xr-x 1 mohit mohit node.sh
It looks like permissions survived. They never went away.

3. userdel vs userdel -r
Command	What it does
userdel mohit	Deletes user from /etc/passwd only. Files in /home/mohit and elsewhere stay.
userdel -r mohit	Deletes user + deletes /home/mohit + mail spool. Other files elsewhere still stay.
Even -r does NOT delete files the user created outside his home, like /home/node.sh or /tmp/script.sh.

4. Why This Matters in Real Jobs
When an employee leaves a company:

Admin runs userdel old_employee
His files become owned by ghost UID 1001
New employee with same UID 1001 would suddenly own all old files!
Fix in production:

# Find all files owned by ghost UID
find / -uid 1000 -ls
# Give them to new owner
chown -R new_user:new_user /home/old_user
